    "updated_at": "2026-10-22T18:26:51Z",
    "body": "Still no supported switch on 2.1.300 / 2.1.301 that I can find. The 2.1.300 bundle does contain an env name `CLAUDE_CODE_AGENT_PROGRESS_SUMMARIES`, but its behaviour isn't documented. Could a maintainer confirm whether it disables `agent_summary`? The refresh schedule in that bundle defaults to `refreshSeconds: [30]` (clamped to 30–240 s) and appears to be server-configured per model family.\n\n**A datapoint without raw request capture.** Because the summary fork runs with `skipTranscript: true`, it can be measured as the tokens Claude Code's own `cost-state` counts but no transcript row carries. On **Fable 5.1** sub-agents, across five sessions (2.1.273–2.1.300, Linux):\n\n- hidden cache reads = **0.7–1.08 ×** Σ(context of every sub-agent message that issued a non-read-only tool call)\n- ≈ **3–4.4 k uncached input tokens** per such message\n\nThat is roughly one full-context re-read per agent step, as expected when each step of a long-running agent takes longer than the 30 s refresh. On one 36-hour session the Fable sub-agents' visible cache reads were 142 M against 133 M hidden. Opus 5.5 was 1,184 M visible against 338 M hidden, though the Opus figure also contains other background requests (auto-mode classifier, compaction).\n\nTwo asks in addition to the switch:\n1. Record the usage of these forks somewhere on disk (see #100830 for why transcript-based accounting is already short).\n2. Let the summary cadence key on activity, so a sub-agent blocked on a ten-minute Bash call is not re-summarised every 30 s.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
